window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"06-01","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"06-01-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-01/","title_he":"א – מצוות ישוב הארץ יסוד האמונה","title_en":"The Mitzva of Settling the Land Is the Foundation of Faith","body_en":"<p>The Sages said: \"Dwelling in the Land of Israel is equal to all the mitzvot in the Torah\" (Tosefta Avoda Zara 4:3; Sifrei Re'eh 53). And in the Torah it is said (Devarim 11:12): \"A land which the Lord your God cares for; the eyes of the Lord your God are always upon it, from the beginning of the year to the end of the year.\" And the Sages said (Ketubot 110b): \"Whoever dwells in the Land of Israel is like one who has a God, and whoever dwells outside the Land is like one who has no God.\" They further said (ibid.): \"Whoever dwells outside the Land is as though he worships idols.\"</p>\n<p>We must understand why the physical land occupies so important a place in the Torah, and what its great importance is for the life of faith, to the point that the Sages said that whoever lives outside the Land is as though he worships idols.</p>\n<p>The answer is that the basic sin of idolatry is that it divides and separates the world into different domains. From observing the different forces in the world, people reached the conception that the roots of the forces too are separate, and therefore there are different gods, each of them ruling its own particular domain. The greatest division that exists in the world, and accordingly in the conception of idolatry, is the division between spirituality and materiality, which at times is considered the division between good and evil. And such is the reality outside the Land: there the Jew can reveal holiness only in spirituality, while estranged from nature, for everything he adds in developing science and the economy may help kingdoms and governments do evil. So it turned out that the Jews who helped Germany develop its economy, science and society, and lay its railway lines, helped them initiate the Second World War and carry out the greatest mass murder – the Holocaust. This is the most terrible historical example, but to some extent this was so in all the exiles. In any case, from this situation it appears as though the word of God can be revealed only in the domains of the spirit, and not within actual earthly reality. And there is no deeper injury to the faith of Israel than to say that He, blessed be He, can be revealed only in the spiritual aspects, as though the whole physical domain exists without Him and even in opposition to Him. Therefore one who dwells outside the Land, in a place where faith is revealed only in the life of the spirit, is considered as one who worships idols and has no God, for the Lord our God is one, and one who lives outside the Land does not connect with the one God who gives life to the heavens and the earth.</p>\n<p>By contrast, the special quality of the Land of Israel is that it is the Holy Land. Although it is a physical land – it is holy, and one can reveal the word of God in its physicality too. For this purpose the Holy One, blessed be He, created it. Therefore it is a mitzva to settle the Land, to build houses in it and to plant trees. And therefore its fruits too are holy, and in the Shemita year we were commanded to make them ownerless for all, and in the other years we were commanded to set aside terumot and ma'asrot from them.</p>\n<p>It follows, then, that in the Land of Israel the unifying faith is revealed, which is the essence and foundation of the faith of Israel, as we say every day, morning and evening: \"Hear, O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is one\" (Devarim 6:4). The Holy One, blessed be He, created the whole world – the heavens, the earth and all that is in them, the spiritual and the physical. And our task is to cleave to God in all areas of life. As the Sages said (Berakhot 63a): \"In all your ways know Him\" (Mishlei 3:6) – this is a small passage on which all the essentials of the Torah depend.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-02/","title_he":"ב – האמונה שמתגלה בטבע","title_en":"The Faith That Is Revealed in Nature","body_en":"<p>Those of narrow faith, in whose conception there is a certain idolatrous aspect, think that faith rests on miracles, and that the more miracles there are, the more faith will grow. Therefore nature is problematic for them, because it disturbs their faith. So they try to describe everything as a miracle. In their shortness of understanding they think that God is revealed only in what is beyond nature, and therefore they try to nullify the real ways of nature. But in truth a grave heresy emerges from their words, according to which nature itself is distant from God, heaven forbid. Science too is a great problem in their eyes, for in their opinion it expresses the greatness of the wisdom in nature at the expense of the miracle. But complete faith is to know that God created the heavens and the earth, and He is the one who gave wisdom to man to develop science, and this is included in the mitzva \"to work it and to guard it\" (Bereshit 2:15), to bring out the forces hidden in nature. It is true that, because there is logic in nature, there are people today who are satisfied with limited explanations without thinking about the Creator and faith, and against them there are people who try to emphasise the miracle. But complete faith sees the whole of nature as divine creation, and is not satisfied with this but aspires to reveal, through the guidance of the Torah, the holy value in everything found in nature.</p>\n<p>From here one can understand the importance and centrality of the mitzva of settling the Land, for this mitzva compels us to reveal all the values of the Torah within earthly reality, with all the real earthly considerations. According to the narrow conception, the mitzva must be revealed without reference to any realistic consideration: if we were commanded to conquer the Land, it must be conquered without any regard for our military ability and the forces standing against us. And since this conception is contrary to logic and cannot be realised, its adherents accordingly claim that the mitzva of settling the Land will be fulfilled only when the Messiah comes, through a revealed miracle beyond every realistic consideration. But the Torah teaches us that in the Land of Israel there is no need of miracles, for holiness is revealed in the Land. In contrast to the signs and wonders revealed in Egypt, in the land of the sons of Ḥam, and in contrast to the miracles revealed afterwards in the wilderness, with the entry into the Land the miracles cease: God no longer provides manna and quail miraculously, shoes and clothes begin to wear out in the way of all the earth, and the pillar of fire and cloud and the well no longer accompany the camp of Israel. For in the Land of Israel one must reveal the holiness that is revealed through nature. And all the miracles that took place in the Land were intended only to point to certain ideas, but they were not such that without them it would be impossible to exist. And so we learned in the book of Numbers that God commanded Israel to prepare for the conquest of the Land, and therefore He commanded Moshe to count all those fit for the army, the men from the age of twenty years and upward.</p>\n<p>Outside the Land, however, we have no choice: since one cannot reveal the divine values in reality, and the people of Israel is in exile and humiliation, only through a miracle that departs from nature can the word of God be seen, and only through a miracle does the people of Israel survive despite the persecutions. And so our master Rav Kook of blessed memory wrote (Orot Ha-teḥiya 28): \"The holiness in nature is the holiness of the Land of Israel, and the Shekhina that went down into exile with Israel is the capacity to establish holiness in opposition to nature. But holiness that fights against nature is not complete holiness, etc.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-03/","title_he":"ג – כלל ישראל בארץ לעומת יחידיות בחו\"ל","title_en":"The Collective of Israel in the Land as Against Individuals Abroad","body_en":"<p>The unifying faith is expressed also in the revelation of the word of God through the collective. Among the nations of the world there are great righteous people, but they are private righteous individuals. But there is no people among the nations of the world that can consolidate as a people for the sake of serving God; for this the people of Israel was chosen, which alone can reveal the holiness of the collective. This is the great vision of revealing complete faith in the world: that a whole people, with all its parts and shades – sages and men of action, kohanim, Levites and Israelites – together will reveal the word of God. This is the holiness of the collective, which exists only in the people of Israel. And this special level of the people of Israel can be revealed only in the Land of Israel, as it is said (II Shmuel 7:23): \"And who is like Your people, like Israel, one nation in the land.\" And the Zohar explained (3:93b) that it is specifically in the Land that the people of Israel is called one nation, and not in any other place (Orot, Mahalakh Ha-ide'ot, chapter 2; Ikvei Ha-tzon, the essay 'Da'at Elohim', p. 136).</p>\n<p>And so we find that all the collective mitzvot are fulfilled only in the Land: so regarding a king (Devarim 17:14-20), so regarding judges and officers (ibid. 16:18-20), so regarding kohanim and Levites, all of whose systems of connection with the people are arranged in the Land of Israel, in the forty-two cities of the Levites and the six cities of refuge (Bamidbar 35); in the terumot, ma'asrot, ḥalla and the other priestly gifts, which we were commanded to give only in the Land. And above all, only in the Land of Israel can the Temple be built, in which is the principal revelation of the Shekhina in the world.</p>\n<p>Since complete faith is revealed in the collective of Israel, the level of prophecy too, which is the highest level a person can reach, depends on the collective of Israel, for a prophet prophesies only for the sake of the collective of Israel (Rashi, Devarim 2:17). And there were great men who were worthy of prophecy, but their generation was not worthy, and therefore they did not prophesy (Sota 48b; Sanhedrin 11a). Likewise prophecy is revealed only in the Land of Israel or for the sake of the Land of Israel, because only in the Land of Israel is the holiness of the collective revealed (Mo'ed Katan 25a; Eikha Rabba, Petiḥta 24; Mekhilta Bo 1; Kuzari 2:14).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-04/","title_he":"ד – הקיום בארץ ובגלות","title_en":"Existence in the Land and in Exile","body_en":"<p>The existence of the people of Israel depends on faith, on its connection with God. Unlike other peoples, which began to develop naturally and afterwards sought meaning and faith for themselves, the people of Israel was formed out of the divine revelation at the Exodus from Egypt and the giving of the Torah, with one aim – to enter the Land and live a life of faith in it. It follows, then, that faith is the life of the people of Israel, and the Land of Israel is called 'the land of life' (Avot de-Rabbi Natan 34:10; Tanḥuma Vayetze 23), because only in it can faith be revealed completely. But outside the Land, as we have learned, it is impossible to reveal the unifying faith. And consequently there is a concern that every attempt to connect with God, blessed be He, will deviate in the direction of idolatry, as our Sages said (Ketubot 110b): \"Whoever dwells outside the Land is as though he worships idols.\"</p>\n<p>Therefore Jewish life outside the Land is called 'exile' (galut), that is, a temporary and unnatural situation, which has no value in itself but only as a situation of waiting, anticipation and preparation to return to the Land (Maharal, Netzaḥ Yisrael chapter 1).</p>\n<p>Seemingly, according to this there would be no place to keep the mitzvot outside the Land; but we were commanded to keep the mitzvot outside the Land so that we will be accustomed to them when we return to the Land. As the Sages said (Sifrei Ekev 37): \"Although I exile you from the Land to outside the Land – be distinguished in the mitzvot, so that when you return they will not be new to you. A parable to a king who was angry with his wife, and she returned to her father's house. He (the king) said to her: Be adorned with your jewellery, so that when you return they will not be new to you. So the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: My children, be distinguished in the mitzvot, so that when you return they will not be new to you. This is what Yirmeyahu said (31:20): 'Set up road-marks (tziyunim) for yourself' – these are the mitzvot by which Israel are distinguished.\" And so it is brought in Rashi (Devarim 11:18): \"'And you shall place these words of Mine' – even after you are exiled (from the Land), be distinguished in the mitzvot: put on tefillin, make mezuzot, so that they will not be new to you when you return.\" It follows that all keeping of the mitzvot outside the Land is so that afterwards we will be able to keep them completely in the Land (see Ramban, Vayikra 18:25). In addition, all the mitzvot dependent on the Land are not fulfilled outside the Land, as will be explained below (3:20).</p>\n<p>And if the people of Israel tries to exist outside the Land without being connected to the Land of Israel, even if it makes efforts to keep Torah and mitzvot, it will not succeed, and in the end it will assimilate and be absorbed among the nations. For the whole existence of the people of Israel in exile depends on the depth of its connection and longing for the Land of Israel. And as our master Rav Kook wrote: \"The anticipation of salvation is the sustaining force of exilic Judaism\" (Orot, Eretz Yisrael 1).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-05/","title_he":"ה – גילוי התורה בארץ","title_en":"The Revelation of the Torah in the Land","body_en":"<p>The principal revelation of the Torah is in the Land of Israel, as we have already learned (above 2) that a prophet prophesies only in the Land of Israel or for the sake of the Land of Israel. And the place of the revelation of the Oral Torah too is the Land of Israel, for \"the Great Court in Jerusalem is the essence of the Oral Torah, and they are the pillar of instruction, and from them statute and judgement go forth to all Israel\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Mamrim 1:1).</p>\n<p>In the Great Court sat seventy-one elders, and to this court, which is also called the Great Sanhedrin, the Torah gave authority to establish enactments and decrees, and it is a positive mitzva for all Israel to heed the instructions of the Great Court, as it is said (Devarim 17:10): \"And you shall act according to the word which they tell you from <b>that place</b> which the Lord shall choose, and you shall take care to do according to all that they instruct you\" (and see the Ramban's commentary there). And one who does not heed them transgresses a negative prohibition (Rambam there 1:1-2). After the Great Court ceased, the authority of the Sages was impaired, and only as a temporary measure did the Sages establish enactments and decrees. More than this, the Great Court had absolute authority to decide disputes between the Sages, and so the Torah was one; and after it ceased, opinions multiplied and the light of the Torah diminished, until the Torah became many separate flocks (Rambam there 1:4).<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>And not only the questions of the collective with which the Great Court dealt are connected to the Land of Israel; even the laws of the individual that come before the court, such as capital cases, fines, lashes and the other laws, depend on the Land of Israel. For the authority of the Sages of Israel to judge all the laws of the Torah depends on 'the ordination of the Sages' (semikhat ḥakhamim), that is, on the ordination with which Moshe our teacher ordained his students to judge the laws of the Torah, and they ordained their students after them, and so the ordination passed from generation to generation. And only in the Land of Israel may a rabbi ordain his student; outside the Land a rabbi may not ordain his student (Rambam, Hilkhot Sanhedrin 4:4). About three hundred years after the destruction of the Second Temple, when the decrees grew harsher, and the Jewish community in the Land became greatly impoverished, and the batei midrash were almost closed, ordination ceased from Israel. And although there were then in Bavel great Torah scholars and enormous batei midrash, it was not possible to continue the ordination, because they were outside the Land. And since then the Sages have no independent authority to judge the laws of the Torah; only in the laws that it is necessary to judge in order to sustain the nation may the Sages judge as the agents of the ordained Sages (Gittin 88b; Bava Kamma 84b; SA ḤM 1:1; 2:1). Thus the existence of the Torah depends on the Land of Israel.</p>\n<p>Even after ordination ceased, and the months are no longer sanctified by the court but are sanctified according to the calculation that the Sages of Israel established, the validity of the calculation depends on the Jews who are in the Land, as it is said (Yeshayahu 2:3): \"For out of Zion shall go forth Torah, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem\" (see Berakhot 63a). And if, heaven forbid, there were some time in which Jews did not live in the Land, the whole calculation of the months would be nullified, and following it all the festivals. But the Holy One, blessed be He, promised Israel that He would not destroy the Israelite nation and its signs, and so there were always Jews in the Land of Israel, and by their merit the months and festivals continued in Israel (Rambam, Hilkhot Kiddush Ha-ḥodesh 5:13; Sefer Ha-mitzvot, positive mitzva 153; Ḥatam Sofer YD 234).</p>\n<p>This is what the Sages said (Ḥagiga 5b): \"Since Israel were exiled from their place – there is no neglect of Torah greater than this.\" And accordingly it is understood that whoever studies Torah in the Land of Israel merits a special blessing, as the Sages said: \"There is no Torah like the Torah of the Land of Israel\" (Bereshit Rabba 16:4), and \"The air of the Land of Israel makes one wise\" (Bava Batra 158b). For in the Land of Israel the inner depth of the Torah is revealed, and therefore most of the revelations of the secrets of the Torah were revealed in the Land. Out of the revelation of the inner dimension the unity of the Torah is revealed, and therefore the Torah scholars of the Land of Israel are pleasant to one another in halakha (Sanhedrin 24a).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"See the book 'Ha-takkanot be-Yisrael' 1, p. 29: some say that the Torah prohibition of 'you shall not turn aside' applies only to the Great Court that sat in the Chamber of Hewn Stone adjacent to the Temple (Yere'im), and some say that the prohibition of 'you shall not turn aside' applies even if it sat in another place (Derashot Ha-Ran 5; Responsa of the Ri Migash 148). And some say that the prohibition of 'you shall not turn aside' applies also to things that the Amora'im enacted (the Rashbatz in Zohar Ha-raki'a, Shoresh 1; the Mabit, introduction to Kiryat Sefer, 5). But it is clear that they limited themselves to enacting only things necessary for the existence of the nation. And see Orot Ha-Torah 1:3."}]},{"slug":"06-01-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-06/","title_he":"ו – מקומה של ארץ ישראל אצל האבות","title_en":"The Place of the Land of Israel for the Patriarchs","body_en":"<p>Having learned about the virtue of the Land of Israel, it is understood why the first divine instruction to the first Jew, Avraham our father, was to go to the Land of Israel, as it is said (Bereshit 12:1): \"And the Lord said to Avram: Go forth from your land and from your birthplace and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you\", for only in the Land will the people of Israel be able to reveal the word of God completely to the world, as it is said (ibid. 2-3): \"And I will make you a great nation, and I will bless you and make your name great, and you shall be a blessing. And I will bless those who bless you, and him who curses you I will curse, and in you shall all the families of the earth be blessed.\" Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, promised Avraham our father (ibid. 13:15-17): \"For all the land which you see, to you will I give it, and to your seed for ever… Arise, walk through the land in its length and in its breadth, for to you will I give it\" (and likewise ibid. 15:7; 17:8).</p>\n<p>Yitzḥak our father had a special virtue. He did not indeed merit to go up to the Land like his father, but his great merit is that he was born in the Land, and he was the first Jew to be circumcised on the eighth day, and therefore he was wholly holy. This came to expression in his being bound upon the altar as a perfect burnt offering. Therefore a special instruction was given to him, to remain in the Land despite the hardships of famine, as it is said (Bereshit 26:2-3): \"And the Lord appeared to him and said: Do not go down to Egypt; dwell in the land of which I tell you. Sojourn in this land, and I will be with you and bless you, for to you and to your seed I will give all these lands, and I will establish the oath which I swore to Avraham your father…\"</p>\n<p>The life of Ya'akov our father was more complicated: he contended with a wicked brother and was forced to flee the Land; but precisely from the complications we learned how deep and eternal the bond of Israel to the Land is. At the time he fled from Esav his brother, after the vision of the ladder, the divine revelation to him concerned the Land of Israel, as it is said (Bereshit 28:13-15): \"The land on which you lie, to you will I give it and to your seed. And your seed shall be as the dust of the earth, and you shall spread abroad to the west and to the east and to the north and to the south, and in you and in your seed shall all the families of the earth be blessed. And behold, I am with you, and I will guard you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land…\" (and likewise ibid. 35:12; 46:4).</p>\n<p>At the end of his days Ya'akov our father was forced to go down to Egypt, to his son Yosef, who had become ruler and viceroy. When he felt that his days were drawing near to die, he called his son Yosef and asked him not to bury him in Egypt. And although Yosef could have arranged a very honourable burial for him in Egypt, Ya'akov asked that they bring him back to the Land, to the graves of his fathers in the Cave of Makhpela. And he was so concerned about this that he demanded that Yosef swear to him about it. And his mind was not at rest until he again spoke about it at length with all his sons. And when he finished speaking about the Cave of Makhpela and the graves of his fathers, Ya'akov our father expired and was gathered to his people. So it turned out that after his passing all his sons went on a long journey on foot to the Cave of Makhpela in Ḥevron, and along the long way they internalised the awareness that their homeland is the Land of Israel, and to it they would return after the days of exile decreed upon them in Egypt came to an end (Bereshit 47:29-31; 48:21; 49:29-32).</p>\n\n<p>This heritage, handed down to the children of Israel from the patriarchs, remained in the hearts of the children of Israel in Egypt. And despite the harsh slavery in Egypt, they always knew that a day would come when the Lord, God of their fathers, would remember them and bring them back to the Land that He had sworn to them. And indeed the purpose of the Exodus from Egypt was to return to the Land of Israel and reveal the word of God in it, as it is said (Shemot 6:8): \"And I will bring you to the land concerning which I lifted up My hand to give it to Avraham, to Yitzḥak and to Ya'akov, and I will give it to you as a heritage; I am the Lord\" (and likewise ibid. 3:8; 3:17; 13:5; 13:11).</p>\n<p>Likewise Yosef the righteous asked to be buried in the Land of Israel. And although he knew that after his death his sons would not have the ability to bury him in the Land, he did not despair of returning to the Land, and he made the children of Israel swear that at the time they were redeemed from Egypt, they would carry his bones with them and bring him back to his homeland, to bury him in his inheritance in the Land of Israel (Bereshit 50:24-25). And indeed when they left Egypt they carried his bones with them in all their journeys, and Moshe our teacher was responsible for this in the wilderness, and when they merited to enter the Land they buried him in Shekhem.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-07/","title_he":"ז – חטא המרגלים","title_en":"The Sin of the Spies","body_en":"<p>Israel had two terrible sins in the wilderness: the sin of the calf, in which they made a molten calf and bowed down to it, and the sin of the spies, in which they were drawn after the counsel of the spies who melted the heart of the people, saying that the people of Israel would not be able to conquer the Land. In several respects the sin of the spies is graver than the sin of the calf. For in the sin of the calf Israel did not deny God and Moshe entirely; they only erred and thought that since Moshe had not returned from Mount Sinai, God would not continue to reveal Himself to them in His glory and in His own Being, and they must seek for themselves an idol that would mediate between them and the Creator. And since they did not deny entirely, after the sin of the calf the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave Israel. But in the sin of the spies they denied God's ability to act in the world and to help them conquer the Land. And they also betrayed the principal mission for which the world was created and Israel were chosen – to reveal the Shekhina in the world through the Land of Israel. Therefore the sin of the spies was not forgiven, and it was decreed on all the partners in the sin that they would die in the wilderness, and only Yehoshua bin Nun and Kalev ben Yefuneh, who rose up against the sinners, merited to enter the Land.</p>\n<p>That night on which they wept and despised the desirable Land was the night of Tisha Be-Av. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: \"You wept a weeping for nothing, and I will establish for you a weeping for generations\" (Sanhedrin 104b). At that time it was decreed that the Temple would be destroyed and that Israel would be exiled from their Land (see Tanḥuma Shelaḥ).</p>\n<p>Seemingly one should ask: in what did the spies sin? In their opinion the people of Israel did not have the ability to conquer the Land. And if the people insisted on fighting, it would be defeated and danger would hover over its existence. If so, a moral obligation rested upon them to warn of the danger, since the existence of the Jewish people is more important than the mitzva of settling the Land. And even if they erred in their assessment of reality, and in truth Israel could have conquered the Land, since they said their words out of inner conviction, in order to save the people from defeat and annihilation, seemingly they should not have been given a severe punishment but should have been praised for the national responsibility they showed.</p>\n<p>But they sinned in that they did not understand the value of the Land and did not love it. And since they did not love the Land, when they saw the difficulties involved in conquering it, their heart fell, and they began to find for themselves reasons and arguments why it was impossible to enter the Land, until in the end they no longer believed in the ability of Israel to conquer it. As it is said (Tehillim 106:24): \"And they despised the desirable land; they did not believe His word.\" By contrast, Yehoshua and Kalev, who loved the Land and said: \"The land is very, very good\" (Bamidbar 14:7), despite the difficulties, saw reality correctly and believed that it was possible to conquer the Land, as they said to the people: \"Let us go up at once and possess it, for we are well able to overcome it\" (ibid. 13:30).</p>\n<p>Indeed it became clear that Yehoshua and Kalev were right. Had they listened to them, the whole generation of the wilderness would have been saved and would have merited to enter the Land. And precisely the spies, who supposedly wanted to look after the welfare of the people so that they would not die in war, caused the death of them all in the wilderness.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The spies thought that the life of the wilderness was more sanctified and elevated, because in it one is not occupied with matters of action and matter, and therefore in their hearts they despised the desirable Land. Similarly wrote the first Rebbe of Ḥabad (Likkutei Torah, Shelaḥ 38b), that they did not want to enter the Land of Israel, and they argued: why must one descend to the world of action, since it is possible to keep \"the Torah and mitzvot spiritually and not in physical deeds\"? \"But in truth they were mistaken, for the main thing is to do them specifically in the Land. As our Sages of blessed memory said (Sota 14a): how many prayers Moshe prayed to enter the Land of Israel! And they asked: did he then need to eat of its fruits? Rather, to fulfil the mitzvot dependent on it.\" For keeping the mitzvot in the Land is the greatest sanctification of God's name, and therefore a great light is revealed from it, more than in spiritual life alone.<br>In recent generations too, when the awakening to the return to Zion and the resettlement of the Land began, leaders arose who tried to dissuade the heart of the children of Israel, arguing that only dangers awaited them in the Land of Israel: dangers of hunger and disease, and dangers from Arab murderousness; and they even accused the immigrants of endangering by their actions the Jewish majority that remained in the diaspora and was working to gain equal rights. In time it became clear that in Zion there was a refuge from the destruction of assimilation and the physical annihilation of millions of Jews who remained in the diaspora.<br>Similarly today, one who does not understand the value of the Land finds that there is difficulty in settling it, and wants to withdraw, and brings disasters upon the people of Israel. And one who understands its value finds practical ways to hold on to the Land and settle it. If we observe, we will find that among those who love the Land there is a large and solid majority that opposes any withdrawal whatsoever, and is sure that we have the ability to hold on to the Land and settle it. Whereas among those who do not love it, there is a majority that is sure there is no possibility of holding on to the Land and that parts of it must be given up. To what may this be compared? To a lazy man whom one tries to persuade to work. He will argue a thousand realistic arguments why it is not worth his while to look for work. First, there is unemployment and no one is looking for workers, and it is a pity to waste time on searching. And if they find him work, he will fear that the employer will cheat him. And in general he will argue that it is better to receive unemployment benefit. And even if we prove to him that if he works he will earn more, he will argue that it is not worth his while to accept the work offered, for perhaps tomorrow they will offer him better work. And if we nevertheless succeed in persuading him to work, he will feel a pain in his back. We will cure his back – he will feel it in his head. We will cure his head – he will quarrel with his fellow workers. We will make peace between them – he will be hurt by the manager's behaviour. And so there will always be things that prevent him from working. The sum of the matter: the problem is that he does not want to work."}]},{"slug":"06-01-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-08/","title_he":"ח – ישוב הארץ כביטוי לגאולה","title_en":"Settling the Land as an Expression of Redemption","body_en":"<p>So important is the mitzva of settling the Land that the situation of the people of Israel depends on its fulfilment. With the destruction of the Land the people was destroyed, and with the ingathering of the exiles and the building of the Land the people is built, and through this the word of God is revealed in the world.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>These things are explicit in the Torah and the Prophets: that the destruction of the Land and the exile of Israel are the sharpest expression of divine punishment, as we say every day in the passage 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' (Devarim 11:13-17): \"And it shall come to pass, if you shall diligently listen to My commandments… that I will give the rain of your land in its season… Take heed to yourselves, lest your heart be deceived, and you turn aside and serve other gods and bow down to them. And the anger of the Lord will be kindled against you, and He will shut up the heavens so that there will be no rain, and the ground will not yield its produce, and you will perish quickly from off the good land which the Lord gives you.\" And likewise it is said (ibid. 29:21-27): \"And they will see the plagues of that land and the sicknesses with which the Lord has made it sick: brimstone and salt, a burning is all its land, it is not sown, nor does it bear, nor does any grass grow in it, like the overthrow of Sedom and Amora, Adma and Tzevoyim, which the Lord overthrew in His anger and in His wrath. And all the nations will say: Why has the Lord done thus to this land? What is the heat of this great anger? And they will say: Because they forsook the covenant of the Lord, the God of their fathers, which He made with them when He brought them out of the land of Egypt… And the anger of the Lord was kindled against that land, to bring upon it all the curse that is written in this book. And the Lord uprooted them from their land in anger and in wrath and in great indignation, and cast them into another land, as it is this day.\" And the redemption is (ibid. 30:3-5): \"Then the Lord your God will turn your captivity and have compassion upon you, and will return and gather you from all the peoples where the Lord your God has scattered you. If your outcasts are at the end of the heavens, from there the Lord your God will gather you, and from there He will take you. And the Lord your God will bring you into the land which your fathers possessed, and you shall possess it, and He will do you good and multiply you more than your fathers.\"</p>\n<p>And likewise it is said in Yirmeyahu (22:8-9) concerning the destruction: \"And many nations shall pass by this city, and they shall say every man to his neighbour: Why has the Lord done thus to this great city? And they shall say: Because they forsook the covenant of the Lord their God, and bowed down to other gods and served them\" (and likewise in I Melakhim 9:8-9).</p>\n<p>And likewise it is said in the Prophets concerning the redemption: \"For I will take you from among the nations, and gather you from all the countries, and bring you into your own land\" (Yeḥezkel 36:24). \"And they shall build houses and inhabit them, and they shall plant vineyards and eat their fruit\" (Yeshayahu 65:21). \"Again you shall plant vineyards on the mountains of Shomron; the planters shall plant and shall enjoy the fruit\" (Yirmeyahu 31:4). \"And the desolate land shall be tilled, whereas it was a desolation in the sight of all who passed by. And they shall say: This land that was desolate has become like the garden of Eden, and the waste and desolate and ruined cities are fortified and inhabited\" (Yeḥezkel 36:34-35). \"And I will turn the captivity of My people Israel, and they shall build the waste cities and inhabit them, and they shall plant vineyards and drink their wine, and they shall make gardens and eat their fruit. And I will plant them upon their land, and they shall no more be uprooted from their land which I have given them, says the Lord your God\" (Amos 9:14-15). These are a few examples of the place of the Land in the Prophets.</p>\n<p>And likewise the Sages said: \"The ingathering of the exiles is as great as the day on which heaven and earth were created\" (Pesaḥim 88a). And likewise Rabbi Abba said (Sanhedrin 98a): \"There is no clearer sign of the end than this, as it is said (Yeḥezkel 36:8): But you, O mountains of Israel, you shall shoot forth your branches and yield your fruit to My people Israel, for they are soon to come.\" And Rashi explained: \"When the Land of Israel gives its fruit generously, then the end will be near, and there is no clearer sign of the end than this.\"</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Rabbi Tzadok Ha-kohen of Lublin explained that it is not by chance that all the extravagant praises of the Land of Israel and love for it are brought at the end of tractate Ketubot. For the whole of tractate Ketubot deals with strengthening the bond and love between the spouses – the man and the woman. And out of this the tractate at its end deals with the bond between the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Congregation of Israel, which has a resemblance to the bond between a man and a woman, as it is said (Yeshayahu 62:4-5): \"You shall no more be termed Forsaken, nor shall your land any more be termed Desolate; but you shall be called Ḥeftzi-va (My delight is in her), and your land Be'ula (Married), for the Lord delights in you, and your land shall be married. For as a young man marries a virgin, so shall your sons marry you, and as the bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so shall your God rejoice over you.\" The exile is a distancing between the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Congregation of Israel, and the return to the Land is the return to the bridal chamber of the Congregation of Israel with the Holy One, blessed be He. By contrast, in tractate Gittin (55b–58a) the words of the Sages are brought about the destruction that led to the exile."}]},{"slug":"06-01-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-09/","title_he":"ט – ברית מילה וירושת הארץ","title_en":"Brit Mila and the Inheritance of the Land","body_en":"<p>There is a special connection between the mitzva of brit mila (circumcision) and the mitzva of settling the Land. Both express the special vision of the people of Israel – to reveal holiness within earthly, physical reality. The world of matter is by its nature closed to receiving spiritual ideas, and this is the meaning of brit mila: to remove the orla (foreskin), which expresses the opacity of materiality, and to enable matter to be a partner in revealing spiritual ideas. And the place in which holiness can be revealed within actual life is the Land of Israel.</p>\n<p>And so we find that at the time the Holy One, blessed be He, raised Avraham our father from the level of a private righteous man to the level of a collective righteous man, and made a covenant with him that not only would he be connected to God, but there would come forth from him a whole nation that would reveal the word of God for all generations – He promised him the Land and commanded him concerning brit mila, sealed in his flesh and sanctifying him. As it is said (Bereshit 17:7-11): \"And I will establish My covenant between Me and you and your seed after you throughout their generations, for an everlasting covenant, to be a God to you and to your seed after you. And I will give to you and to your seed after you the land of your sojournings, all the land of Kena'an, for an everlasting possession, and I will be their God. And God said to Avraham: And as for you, you shall keep My covenant, you and your seed after you throughout their generations. This is My covenant, which you shall keep, between Me and you and your seed after you: every male among you shall be circumcised. And you shall circumcise the flesh of your foreskin, and it shall be a sign of the covenant between Me and you.\"</p>\n<p>This is what the Sages said (Bereshit Rabba 46:9): \"If your children keep circumcision – they enter the Land, and if not – they do not enter the Land.\" Therefore Yehoshua was commanded to circumcise Israel before the beginning of the conquest of the Land, as it is said (Yehoshua 5:2): \"At that time the Lord said to Yehoshua: Make for yourself flint knives, and circumcise the children of Israel again a second time.\" For in those forty years that Israel were in the wilderness on account of the sin of the spies, they did not circumcise themselves. But when they entered the Land, Yehoshua said to them: \"What do you think, that you will enter the Land uncircumcised? Thus said the Holy One, blessed be He, to Avraham our father: And I will give to you and to your seed after you the land, etc. – on condition that: And as for you, you shall keep My covenant\" (Bereshit Rabba 46:9).<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>An interesting historical fact is that a people whose sons are not circumcised is unable to settle permanently in the Land of Israel. And so our Sages said (Zohar 2:23a): whoever is circumcised can inherit the Land. They further said: the sons of Yishmael are destined to rule over the Holy Land for a long time, while it is empty and desolate, and this is because Yishmael was circumcised, and they will prevent the children of Israel from returning to their place. But because their circumcision is empty, without completeness (they do not circumcise on the eighth day, and also do not uncover the thin skin, and whoever circumcises and does not uncover it is as though he did not circumcise), therefore the Land too will be empty and desolate under them. And in the end Israel, whose circumcision is complete, will merit it (see Zohar 2:32a).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Of both mitzvot the Sages said that they are equal to all the mitzvot: of dwelling in the Land of Israel in Tosefta Avoda Zara 4:3 and Sifrei Re'eh 53, and of circumcision in Nedarim 32a. However, as will be explained below 3:6, from the halakhic aspect the status of the Land of Israel is more important, for one sets aside a rabbinic Shabbat prohibition (shevut) for the sake of settling the Land, and one does not set aside a shevut on Shabbat for the sake of circumcision."}]},{"slug":"06-01-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-10/","title_he":"י – שבת והארץ","title_en":"Shabbat and the Land","body_en":"<p>We find a special connection between the mitzva of Shabbat and the Land of Israel. Shabbat is the sanctified time, the Land of Israel is the sanctified place, and the people of Israel is the sanctified people. And this holiness is not for itself; rather, from the holiness of the Shabbat day an abundance of blessing flows to all the days of the week (Zohar 2:88a), and from the holiness of the Land the whole world is blessed (see Ta'anit 10a), and from the holiness of Israel all the nations are blessed, as it is said (Bereshit 22:18): \"And in your seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed.\"</p>\n<p>The number seven hints at the holiness that exists within nature, for the six days of the week hint at the six sides of every physical thing – four directions, up and down. And Shabbat is the inner dimension of the six sides. Therefore the world was created in six days, and Shabbat came to give the inner meaning of the world (Maharal). And this is also the meaning of the Land of Israel: to reveal holiness within physical reality.</p>\n<p>In order to understand the holiness of the Land one must keep Shabbat. As the Sages said (Bereshit Rabba 46:9): \"If your children accept Shabbat – they enter the Land, and if not – they do not enter.\" For on Shabbat every single Jew is freed from the servitude of work and connects to the remembrance of the creation of the world and the Exodus from Egypt, and through this he can understand the value of the Land of Israel – to reveal the word of God in the world.</p>\n<p>Therefore even the building of the holiest place – the Temple – does not override Shabbat (Yevamot 6a). The Sages learned this from the verse (Vayikra 19:30): \"You shall keep My Sabbaths and revere My Sanctuary; I am the Lord.\" Out of the holiness of Shabbat we come to understand the value of the Temple, and therefore the building of the Temple does not override Shabbat. It follows that by virtue of keeping Shabbat we can understand the holiness of the place, and through this the Land will endure in our hands.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"However, a war of mitzva overrides Shabbat, as it is said (Devarim 20:20): \"until it falls\" – even on Shabbat (Shabbat 19a). And likewise Yeriḥo was conquered on Shabbat (Yerushalmi Shabbat 1:8). But the building of the Land does not override Shabbat, except for the shevut of telling a gentile, where they were lenient to buy a house from a gentile on Shabbat for the sake of settling the Land (SA OḤ 307:5)."}]},{"slug":"06-01-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-11/","title_he":" יא – קדושת הארץ ושנת השמיטה","title_en":"The Holiness of the Land and the Shemita Year","body_en":"<p>The mitzvot of Shemita and Yovel express the holiness of the Land of Israel. Once in seven years the Shemita year falls, and the fruits that grow in that year in the Land of Israel are holy and ownerless for all, and poor and rich may eat of them equally. And in the Yovel year, after seven Shemitot, in the fiftieth year, not only are the fruits sanctified and ownerless for all, but also the fields that were sold during the fifty years return to their owners, and the servants who were sold go out to freedom. Just as Shabbat reveals the holiness of the whole week in the life of the individual, so Shemita and Yovel reveal holiness in the life of the collective (see our master Rav Kook's introduction to 'Shabbat Ha'aretz').</p>\n<p>Through Shemita and Yovel we remember that the whole Land belongs to God, and all the children of Israel are His children, and therefore it is fitting that they be free men. But when Israel do not keep Shemita and Yovel, they defile the Land by exploiting its soil for the sake of their wealth without remembering its destiny, and the Land vomits them out. As the Sages said (Midrash Aggada on Vayikra 26:34): \"The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Sow six and release the seventh, so that you will know that the Land is Mine and I gave it to you as a heritage, as it is said (Vayikra 23:10): When you come into the land which <b>I</b> give you – not by your sword and not by your bow, but <b>I give it to you</b>. And they did not do so, but sinned and did not rest the Shemitot and Yovelot, and caused exile for themselves, as it is said: All the days of its desolation it shall rest.\" And likewise the Sages said (Shabbat 33a) that on account of the sin of Shemita and Yovel, exile comes to the world. And since Israel did not keep seventy years of Shemita and Yovel, they were exiled seventy years to Bavel (Rashi on Vayikra 26:35).</p>\n<p>And while we are in exile, against our will we cannot work the holy soil, and accordingly we will no longer use it as an ordinary land; then the Land will go on and be purified, and in the process the sanctified memories will return to flood the hearts of Israel, and once again the attitude to it will be as to a sanctified place, and by this merit we will return to the Land. As it is said: \"Then shall the land be paid her Sabbaths, all the days of its desolation, while you are in the land of your enemies; then shall the land rest and be paid her Sabbaths. All the days of its desolation it shall rest, the rest which it did not have in your Sabbaths when you dwelt upon it\" (Vayikra 26:34-35). \"And the land shall be forsaken of them, and shall be paid her Sabbaths while she lies desolate without them, and they shall pay for their iniquity…\" (ibid. 43). \"And I will remember for them the covenant of the first ones, whom I brought out of the land of Egypt in the sight of the nations, to be their God; I am the Lord\" (ibid. 45). This prophecy was indeed fulfilled when, after seventy years of the Babylonian exile, Israel began to return to the Land: \"To fulfil the word of the Lord by the mouth of Yirmeyahu, until the land had been paid her Sabbaths; all the days of its desolation it rested, to fulfil seventy years\" (II Divrei Ha-yamim 36:21).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-01-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-01-12/","title_he":"יב – חירות בארץ – תפילין ושחרור עבדים","title_en":"Freedom in the Land – Tefillin and the Freeing of Servants","body_en":"<p>When the people of Israel is in its Land, the soul can be revealed, for the earthly and material side of the Land of Israel does not block the soul from being revealed. And through the revelation of the soul all the children of Israel become free men. For as long as materialism is primary, the poor are enslaved to the rich and the weak to the strong, and the rich and strong are enslaved to their desires. And when the soul is revealed, the trait of the inner freedom of Israel is revealed: that Israel are servants of God and not of any other created being. As it is said (Vayikra 25:55): \"For to Me the children of Israel are servants; they are My servants whom I brought out of the land of Egypt; I am the Lord your God.\" Therefore there is no permanent servitude in Israel; rather, in the fiftieth year, which is the Yovel year, all the servants are freed, and all the inheritances that were sold return to their owners. In this the intrinsic value of every Jew is revealed: he has a portion in the Land of Israel and cannot be a permanent servant, because at his foundation he is a free man.</p>\n<p>Therefore the mitzva of freeing servants is fateful for our existence in the Land of Israel. An ordinary servant is freed after six years, and a servant whose ear was pierced – at the Yovel. And this was one of the sins on account of which the king of Bavel came upon Jerusalem to besiege it and destroy it. And when the princes of Jerusalem saw that evil had been determined against them, they listened to the rebuke of Yirmeyahu and freed their servants. And a miracle happened to them: the king of Bavel lifted the siege from Jerusalem and went to fight Egypt. But they returned to their wickedness and again enslaved their servants, and the king of Bavel returned to besiege Jerusalem and destroyed it (Yirmeyahu 34).</p>\n<p>This is also the foundation of the connection of the mitzva of tefillin to the inheritance of the Land. The mitzva of tefillin too expresses the freedom of Israel, for the tefillin are a sign of our absolute bond to the Holy One, blessed be He. And through the absolute bond to the Holy One, blessed be He, we become free men, freed from every other yoke in the world. Therefore the tefillin are like a crown for Israel. And therefore the subject of the Land of Israel is mentioned many times in the tefillin. In the passage 'Kadesh': \"And it shall be, when the Lord brings you into the land of the Kena'ani, the Ḥitti, the Emori, the Ḥivi and the Yevusi, which He swore to your fathers to give you, a land flowing with milk and honey…\" (Shemot 13:5). In the passage 'Ve-haya ki yevi'akha': \"And it shall be, when the Lord brings you into the land of the Kena'ani, as He swore to you and to your fathers, and gives it to you\" (ibid. 11). The passage 'Ve-haya im shamo'a' deals entirely with the Land of Israel, for all the reward and punishment mentioned in it and in the whole Torah has its principal fulfilment in the Land of Israel. And in the passage 'Shema' the subject of faith in the one God is mentioned, and as we have learned (above 1-2), the principal revelation of the faith of unity is in the Land of Israel.</p>\n<p>The mitzva of tefillin too, like the mitzva of settling the Land, binds holiness into physical reality. For we were commanded to write the holy passages of the tefillin on the hide of an animal, and we place them inside housings of animal hide, to say that even the animal sides are connected to holiness. And this is what the Sages said (Kiddushin 37b): \"Do this mitzva (tefillin), for by its merit you will enter the Land.\"</p>\n<p>And like the Land of Israel, the tefillin too express the unique bond between Israel and God, and therefore one binds the tefillin to the head and to the arm, to express by this that we are actually bound to the divine destiny. And the Sages said (Berakhot 6a) that the Holy One, blessed be He, too puts on tefillin, and in the tefillin of the Holy One, blessed be He, is written: \"And who is like Your people Israel, one nation in the land\" (I Divrei Ha-yamim 17:21).<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"As of the Land of Israel, so too of the mitzva of tefillin the Sages said in Menaḥot 43b that it is equal to all the mitzvot, for it too, like the Land of Israel, expresses the special quality of Israel, and it too comes to bind holiness into physical reality. And in Shevu'ot 29a they said of the mitzva of tzitzit that it is equal to all the mitzvot. For it too, like the mitzva of settling the Land, comes to reveal holiness in reality: the tallit is like matter, and the tzitzit that go out and peer from it hint at the mitzvot that reveal holiness out of matter. And like the tallit that wraps the person who wears it, so and more so the Land of Israel surrounds the one who dwells in it.<br>The Sages further said in Bava Batra 9a of the mitzva of tzedaka that it is equal to all the mitzvot, and likewise in the Yerushalmi, Pe'a 1:1, of gemilut ḥasadim. And likewise we find in the Land of Israel the mitzvot of Shemita and Yovel, which are like a general tzedaka and gemilut ḥasadim. And in the revelation of holiness in the Land there is also an aspect of kindness to materiality, which has become distant from its divine source, and through the revelation of the holiness in it, it returns to connect with its source.<br>And likewise they said of the mitzva of Torah study that it is equal to all the mitzvot (Mishna Pe'a 1:1, and other places). And it has a connection to the Land of Israel, as we learned in halakha 2, that prophecy depends on the Land of Israel, and likewise the Oral Torah depends on the Land of Israel, as we learned in halakha 4.<br>It follows that the seven mitzvot of which the Sages said that they are equal to all the mitzvot are connected and related to the mitzva of settling the Land. Below 3:6 it will be explained that from the halakhic aspect there is an advantage to the mitzva of settling the Land over the other mitzvot of which they said that they are equal to all the mitzvot."}]}]});
